jessica: Heads up — customer_acct_42 is hitting 400s on OpenAI-compatible function/tool calls after a recent change. They say it used to work. Pasting their request + error below.

customer_acct_42: We're calling /v1/chat/completions with functions and tool_call. Response is 400 'tool_call_schema_mismatch'. Model: gpt-openai-compat-1.0. Full request: ```{
  "model": "gpt-openai-compat-1.0",
  "messages": [{"role":"user","content":"charge customer"}],
  "functions": [{
    "name": "charge",
    "parameters": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "id": {"type": "string"},
        "amount": {"type": "number"}
      },
      "required": ["id", "amount"]
    }
  }],
  "tool_call": {
    "name": "charge",
    "arguments": "id=123,amount=10"  
  }
}```

jessica: Error from runtime: 400 - unrecognized enum value "ok" for field "tool_response.status" and schema validator also flagged "arguments" type: expected object but got string. Customer shows their downstream service returns status:"ok" and wraps output as "payload" instead of "result".

liam: Thanks — two separate failures here: (1) enum/envelope mismatch between OpenAI-style tool response and the customer's tool implementation; (2) args type mismatch (CSV string vs JSON object). Can you confirm the exact tool response the customer's service returns?

customer_acct_42: Example tool response we receive back from their service (from logs): ```{
  "tool_response": {
    "status": "ok",
    "payload": {"charge_id": "ch_ABC"}
  }
}``` The older integration tolerated "ok" and "payload", but our stricter OpenAI-compat normalizer now enforces status in ["success","failure"] and expects "result" top-level.

arturo: That explains it. Our compat normalizer currently enforces the OpenAI-style envelope strictly ("result", "status" enum), so any alias hits a 400. For hosted customers we can add a compatibility mapping but we should avoid global silent acceptance of arbitrary enums.

nate: Proposed plan: add a per-customer compatibility toggle plus a small alias mapping table (e.g., "ok" -> "success", "payload" -> "result") that runs in the runtime normalizer. I'll open a PR but won't enable global acceptance without opt-in. For short-term: give the customer a workaround and a repro curl.

jessica: Sending workaround now. Suggested curl (makes arguments a JSON object and demonstrates expected envelope): ```bash
curl -sS -X POST https://api.redwood.example/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer <KEY>' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model":"gpt-openai-compat-1.0",
    "messages":[{"role":"user","content":"charge"}],
    "functions":[{"name":"charge","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"amount":{"type":"number"}},"required":["id","amount"]}}],
    "tool_call": {"name":"charge", "arguments": {"id":"123", "amount": 10} }
  }'```

liam: I'll update the validator to provide a clearer error message when arguments is a string that looks like comma-separated key-values (detect and suggest conversion). Also add a hint for enum aliasing: "Received \"ok\" - did you mean \"success\"? See docs: https://redwood.dev/docs/tool-call-compat"

arturo: I'm going to add a short note in the docs about envelope differences (output/payload/result) and enum aliases. We'll recommend customers either conform to the OpenAI envelope or set the compatibility header `X-Redwood-Compat: openai-toolcall-loose` to enable runtime aliasing for legacy integrations.

nate: Created internal ESCAL ticket ESCL-2026 and opened PR: https://github.com/redwood/inference/pull/837 — targets runtime normalizer with per-customer mappings, telemetry, and opt-in header. No autopromote to hosted until QA.

support-bot: Incident ESCL-2026 created and pinned logs: https://internal.redwood/ESCL-2026 (internal)

jessica: Sending customer the repro + optional header workaround. If they can change their tool to return arguments as JSON object and status 'success' we'll be green. Otherwise we'll enable a compat mapping for their account behind the opt-in header.

customer_acct_42: We can change quickly on our side to return a JSON object and use "success". Appreciate the clear repro and the temporary header option.

liam: Follow-up actions: (1) docs update with examples and common alias table; (2) validator UI improvement for better error messaging; (3) runtime PR for per-customer mapping and opt-in header. I'll own 2. Arturo owns 1. Nate owns 3.

arturo: Adding docs draft now and will link in the PR.

jessica: Thanks team. Closing the loop with the customer now. :thumbsup:
